<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Parisian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paris is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and has long been one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s principal political and cultural centres. It was the seat of government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> and the site of treaties such as the Treaty of Paris of 1763, which confirmed British colonial dominance. Its university was an early centre for the study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, and the last Brazilian emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a>, died in exile there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris.jpg" alt="Paris" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yann Caradec from Paris, France · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Tour_Eiffel_vue_de_la_Tour_Saint-Jacques,_Paris_ao%C3%BBt_2014_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Paris lies in northern central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in a north-bending arc of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, whose crest includes two islands, the Île Saint-Louis and the larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite">Île de la Cité</a>, which form the oldest part of the city. The Seine's mouth on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> is about 375 kilometres downstream. The city is relatively flat, with its lowest point 35 metres above sea level, and the highest of its hills, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmartre">Montmartre</a>, rises to 130 metres. Excluding the outlying parks, it covers an oval of about 87 square kilometres, enclosed by the 35-kilometre ring road, the Boulevard Périphérique, and since an annexation in 1860 it has had 20 arrondissements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Parisii, a Gallic tribe, inhabited the Paris area from around the middle of the 3rd century BCE. A major north–south trade route crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite">Île de la Cité</a>, which gradually became an important trading centre, and the Parisii traded with river towns as far away as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> and minted their own coins.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> conquered the Paris Basin for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in 52 BCE, and the Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutetia">Lutetia</a> grew on the Left Bank into a prosperous city with a forum, baths, temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and an amphitheatre. By the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> it was known as Parisius. According to legend, Saint Denis, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Paris, introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the middle of the 3rd century CE and was beheaded on the hill later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmartre">Montmartre</a>.</p><p>For almost all of its history, except for a few brief periods, Paris was governed directly by representatives of the king, emperor or president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In the 17th century the Académie royale de peinture et de sculpture was established in 1648, and urbanism laws have been strictly controlled since the early 17th century. Between 1853 and 1870 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">Prefect</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-eugene-haussmann">Baron Haussmann</a>, rebuilt the city centre, creating wide boulevards, standard facades of cream-grey Paris stone and major parks around the centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Paris is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, with a city population of 2.04 million and a metropolitan population of 13.3 million. The National Assembly granted it municipal autonomy in 1974, and Jacques Chirac became its first elected mayor in March 1977. Its population peaked at 2.9 million in 1921. In the 19th and early 20th centuries artists including Manet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Monet</a> and Renoir worked there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/impressionism">Impressionism</a>, Cubism and Art Deco all evolved in the city. It hosts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>, whose headquarters have been there since November 1958, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oecd">OECD</a>, and it has hosted the Summer Olympics in 1900, 1924 and 2024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Levon V was given safe passage and went to Castile to seek John I's help, moved to France and died in exile in <b>Paris</b> in 1393; in 1396 his title passed to his cousin James I of Cyprus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He died in exile in <b>Paris</b> in 1891, having presided over one of the most stable and constitutional governments of nineteenth-century Latin America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Viking siege of <b>Paris</b> (885–886) he bought the attackers off rather than fight, an act widely seen as cowardice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It sent James M. Mason to London and John Slidell to <b>Paris</b>, but neither won recognition or military aid.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…by Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, Edvard Beneš and the Slovak astronomer and general Milan Rastislav Štefánik, whose Czechoslovak National Council in <b>Paris</b> won Allied recognition in 1918, helped by the fame of the Czechoslovak Legion, which had fought its way along the Trans-Siberian Railway during the Russian Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy was a largely rural land of grain fields, forests and vineyards, crossed by the Saône and by routes linking <b>Paris</b> and Champagne with Lyon, the Alpine passes and Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
